          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Asthma with an acute exacerbation: is that one code, or a code plus something else?",
          "answer": "The retrieved coding notes suggest asthma is usually coded with **two lookups** rather than one code. The notes say the asthma category (J45) has a severity or exacerbation part and a separate part. (Source: *Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes*.)\n\nThe notes also say that **acute exacerbation and status asthmaticus are alternatives, not stacked**. If the record documents status asthmaticus, the code says that and does not also say acute exacerbation. (Same source.)\n\nIf severity is not documented, the notes say the \"unspecified\" asthma code is the correct choice. The coder cannot infer severity from treatment, peak-flow readings, or other parts of the chart. (Same source.)\n\nThe passages did not give the specific J45 code numbers for asthma with acute exacerbation, and the opening part of the asthma document was cut off in what I received. So I can't confirm the exact codes or the full structure from these results. A certified medical coder or the official ICD-10-CM tabular list would give you the definitive answer.\n\nIf you are asking because someone is having an asthma attack and struggling to breathe, seek emergency care now. Do not wait for a coding answer.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
